// logos.std.wql.join_order — WHICH ORDER DRIVES THE JOIN (ADR 0024 S4).
//
// A left-deep join has a BASE — the outermost loop, read once, scanned whole —
// and STEPS, each indexed or rescanned per bound row. Until this module existed
// the base was whatever the query wrote first, and that is the one join decision
// where a STATIC answer cannot be right: which side is cheaper to index depends
// on how many rows each side holds, and no compile-time fact says. That is the
// difference between this axis and the ACCESS axis, where a runtime decision is
// nearly always the same answer (a narrowing over an ordered source is never
// meaningfully worse than a scan — one descent against zero, never more rows).
//
// So the answer is not a better compile-time guess. It is a SET of orders in the
// emitted code and a DISCRIMINANT the plan owns: the sizes the plan asked for
// (S4g) are compared once, and the branch taken is the cheapest order.
//
// ⚠ THAT COMPARISON IS NOT MADE HERE AND NOT IN THE QUERY'S BODY. It is made in
// the query's PREPARE fn and recorded on the plan object (ADR 0024 S4i,
// `logos.std.wql.prepared`) — decided once, applied many times, which is what
// makes it a plan rather than a per-call test. What this module hands over is
// therefore the size EXPRESSIONS, the ADMISSIBLE SET, and the ground.
//
// ⚠ AND THERE ARE TWO MOMENTS TO HAND THEM OVER FOR (ADR 0024 S4j). S4i read its
// own tightening too widely: a size that only exists after the query's prelude
// has run is unusable IN `prepare`, and that is all it means. Inside `run`, past
// the prelude and before the join's index, a materialized rel's `.len()` is the
// same field read the nest's own loop condition makes — free. So this module
// answers TWICE, in order: first with what `prepare` can have (`size_expr_of`),
// and where that fails, with what `run` can have for nothing
// (`run_size_expr_of`). A refusal survives only where NEITHER point has a number,
// which after that slice means exactly one thing: a side that STREAMS, whose
// length would cost draining the iterator — the query's own work traded for a
// plan fact, the error this axis exists to avoid.
//
// ── BEYOND THE FIRST PAIR (ADR 0024 S4k) ─────────────────────────────────────
// S4h–S4j transposed the FIRST PAIR and compared the two candidates by one number
// each. With three or more sources that stopped being a decision: it compared two
// members of a space and it compared them on a fact that does not distinguish the
// rest of it. What this module does now is DERIVE the admissible set and hand the
// whole set over, priced by ONE cost function that lives in `join_cost` and is
// called by the generated plan at run time.
//
// THE CONSTRAINT SET, and every one of them is derived from what the emitter
// actually requires — an illegal order does not crash, it silently returns
// different rows:
//
//   C1 A TOTAL SORT IS THE LICENCE. Deem's default row order IS the nest's:
//      `from a x join b y` yields a-major, and every e2e expectation in the tree
//      reads that way. Driving from `b` yields the same multiset in b-major order
//      — the same rows, a different sequence, which is a semantic change and
//      exactly the failure mode that does not crash. `order by` collects the
//      matched row-index tuples and sorts them, and with the tuple itself as the
//      tiebreak (in QUERY source order — `sort_perm_frag`) the sorted sequence is
//      the same whichever nest produced it. Without `order by` there is nothing to
//      restore the sequence to, and the plan says so instead of reordering anyway.
//
//      ⚠ AND THE CLAIM IS ABOUT THE KEY'S TYPE, NOT ABOUT THE PRESENCE OF
//      `order by`. The tiebreak is guarded by an EQUAL compare, so a key whose
//      comparison is only PARTIAL never reaches it: on an f64 key carrying a NaN
//      both `prev > cur` and `prev == cur` are false, the NaN row's placement (and
//      every placement the halted shift leaves behind it) becomes a fact about
//      WHICH NEST COLLECTED THE TUPLES, and two carried nests return two different
//      sequences from the same data — measured, four orders and four sequences.
//
//      ⚠⚠ AND THE ANTECEDENT USED TO BE NAMED "IS THE CLASS TOTALLY ORDERED",
//      WHICH IS THE WRONG QUESTION AND ADMITTED A WRONG ANSWER. For a u64 key the
//      emitted comparison IS total — and it is the SIGNED reinterpretation, which
//      ranks 2^63 below 0. Every nest agreed on it, so this constraint could not
//      have caught it and the trace said nothing. What C1 needs is that the
//      EMITTED comparison REALIZES THE ORDER THE QUERY NAMED; totality is one of
//      its conditions. That is `el::el_order_faithful`, answered once for every
//      consumer of the sort and keyed on the TYPE NAME because the four-value
//      class tag cannot carry the fact (`u64` and `i64` are one class). A key type
//      that fails it refuses the reorder HERE, with its ground on the trace — the
//      sort itself is unchanged, because what f64 `order by` MEANS for a single
//      nest is a separate question and removing a licence is not the place to
//      answer it. The u64 case is not refused here at all: it is refused where the
//      lossy normalization is introduced (`codegen::check_calls`), so a reorder
//      decision never has to compensate for a value the emitter damaged.
//
//      ⚠ WHAT THIS ANTECEDENT CAN ACTUALLY REFUSE, so the guard is not read as
//      protecting more than it does. On a query that BUILDS it refuses two kinds:
//      PARTIAL (an f64/f32 key) and UNNAMED (a key whose DECLARED type name is out
//      of the lattice — `infer_ty_name` returns the recorded name verbatim, and
//      `ElTypes`' flat field dict is keyed on the field NAME, so a same-named
//      non-scalar field of another source lands there and arrives here). Both are
//      exercised by wql_join_order_key_fidelity_e2e. WRAP/TRUNC arrive only from a
//      declared u64/u128 column or field, whose query cannot build at all — the
//      emitted key vector is `Vec<i64>` and will not take the value — so this is
//      not the guard that stops them; `codegen::check_calls` is, at the one place
//      that used to normalize such a value into i64 silently.
//
//   C2 A PLAN MUST HAVE DECIDED THE CHAIN. A step whose strategy is JS_NONE was
//      never looked at by a planner (a rel body's own chain is emitted per
//      fixpoint variant), so there is no decision to offer a second order for and
//      no recorded strategy to reason from.
//
//   C3 A PINNED STEP KEEPS ITS DEPTH IN THE BOUND STREAM. An ANTI step's
//      predicate is inseparable from its source — "rows of a with no matching b"
//      is not "rows of b with no matching a" — and a TRAVERSAL step's source is a
//      FIELD PATH of an outer row, so it cannot be an outermost loop at all:
//      there is nothing to bind its base to. Both are therefore PINNED: they may
//      not be the base, and they are re-inserted after exactly as many floatable
//      sources as preceded them in the query. What still has to be checked per
//      candidate is C4.
//
//   C4 EVERY SLOT'S DEPENDENCIES PRECEDE IT. A pinned step's `on` and (for a
//      traversal) its SOURCE EXPRESSION reference row vars; those vars must be
//      bound earlier in the candidate. This is the constraint that does the work
//      on real chains — it is what makes a 4-source chain whose anti join depends
//      on the query's base admit no other order at all.
//
//   C5 `on` MOVES WITH THE TRANSPOSITION, AND ONE STEP CARRIES ONE. The predicate
//      is stored on a step but it constrains the PAIR, so in a candidate order
//      each floating predicate attaches to the LAST of the vars it references —
//      the earliest position at which it is evaluable. An order in which two
//      predicates land on one position is INADMISSIBLE, because a step carries one
//      `on` and nothing here builds a conjunction; an order in which a predicate
//      lands on the BASE is inadmissible for the same reason the base carries no
//      predicate: nothing has bound its other side.
//
//   C6 A CANDIDATE MAY NOT TURN A READ-ONCE SOURCE INTO A RESCANNED ONE. HASH and
//      TREE read a step's source exactly once and LOOP rescans it
//      (`join_sel::js_reads_once`), and `plan_walker` decided materialize-vs-
//      stream against the strategies the query's own order got. A candidate that
//      degrades one of them would have the emitted nest read a source the plan may
//      have left as a consumed iterator — which does not run slowly, it silently
//      returns fewer rows.
//
//   C7 A CANDIDATE THAT CANNOT BE CHEAPER IS NOT A CANDIDATE. Two orders whose
//      (size expression, role) sequences are equal cost the same for every
//      possible data, so the second is a nest no measurement could ever select.
//      A self-join is the pair-shaped instance: both sides are the same binding,
//      so the two sizes are equal by construction.
//
// ⚠ THE FIRST STEP'S PIN IS STILL CHECKED AHEAD OF THE DERIVATION, and that is a
// deliberate boundary rather than an oversight. This axis is entered on the first
// pair: when the query's own first join is an anti or a traversal, the pair it
// would transpose does not exist, and the general derivation reaches the same
// conclusion by C4 in every shape the corpus contains (the pinned step's `on`
// references the base's var, so the base is forced and only the query's own order
// survives). Keeping the specific antecedent means a reader of the trace is told
// WHICH property of their query stopped the reorder instead of being told that
// one order survived.
//
// ⚠ WHAT THE PLAN DOES NOT KNOW: nothing in this compiler reports a join's
// SELECTIVITY, so `join_cost` assumes a fixed one and says so. And a pinned step
// is UNPRICED — a traversal's field-path length and an anti's filtered fraction
// are facts no source declares.
//
// ── THE GROUND IS DERIVED, NOT WRITTEN DOWN (ADR 0024 S4m) ───────────────────
// Two of the verdicts this module records were constants, and both of them asserted
// something the derivation could have computed differently:
//
//   • the DEFERRED admission said both sizes were lengths of relations the query
//     materializes anyway — false whenever a side is a PARAMETER, which
//     `size_expr_of` measures happily at point (1). It is composed now, from the
//     per-side classification the deferral was decided on (`prep_size_reason`).
//
//   • a REFUSAL said `first_rej` — the ground of the FIRST permutation that failed,
//     which with three or more sources is one antecedent of a conjunction chosen by
//     enumeration order. It is composed now, from the whole census (`rjn`).
//
// The rule this leaves behind: a `why` may be a literal only where the antecedent it
// names is a literal too. Everything a candidate, a side or a count can vary is
// composed from the same facts the decision used, into the caller's buffer, so the
// justification cannot drift from the decision without the decision changing.
package logos.std.wql.join_order;

use logos.lang.str;
use logos.mem.string;
use logos.lang.writ.container;    // Writ
use logos.lang.writ.anyval;       // WAny
use logos.std.wql.ir;             // SExpr / WRef
use logos.std.wql.el;             // ElTypes
use logos.std.wql.codegen;        // plan_trace / string_as_str — the ONE decision channel
use logos.std.wql.params;         // MacroParams — the rel registry (sizes, streaming)
use logos.std.wql.optimize;       // refs_mask — which vars a predicate reads
use logos.std.wql.join_sel;       // decide_join_step / js_reads_once / js_name / StepSel
use logos.std.wql.join_cost;      // THE cost model: roles, bounds, weights
use logos.std.wql.prepared;       // defer_not_held — the deferred half's exclusions, in ONE copy

// ── The chain, as this module sees it ────────────────────────────────────────
// SLOT 0 is the base and slot i+1 the i-th step, so one index space covers the
// whole nest and a permutation is a permutation of slots. The emitter owns the
// richer `JChain` (row types, byval flags, the recorded key/sel triples); what a
// join-order derivation needs is the source text, the row var, the predicate, the
// two pin flags and the recorded strategy — copied in rather than reached for, so
// this module cannot accidentally depend on an emitter detail.
pub struct JCh {
    pub n:     i64,
    pub src:   [str; 9],
    pub var:   [str; 9],
    pub onw:   [i64; 9],
    pub anti:  [bool; 9],
    pub edge:  [bool; 9],
    pub strat: [i32; 9],
    pub key:   [i32; 9],
    pub sel:   [i64; 9],
    pub swhy:  [str; 9],
}

pub fn jch_new() -> JCh {
    return JCh { n: 0i64,
                 src:  ["", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", ""],
                 var:  ["", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", ""],
                 onw:  [0i64; 9],
                 anti: [false; 9],
                 edge: [false; 9],
                 strat: [0i32; 9],
                 key:   [0i32; 9],
                 sel:   [-1i64; 9],
                 swhy: ["", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", ""] };
}

// ── The decision ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `ncand` — HOW MANY NESTS the artifact carries. 1 means the query's own order and
// no discriminant (no code growth for a query that cannot use a second one);
// 2 is the pair case this generalizes; up to `JC_MAX_CAND()`.
//
// `dynamic` is `ncand > 1` — kept as its own field because it is what every
// consumer actually asks.
//
// `why` is the ground either way, because "the plan left this order alone" is the
// answer a person reading a slow join needs and it is the one a decision recorded
// only on success cannot give.
//
// `nperm` / `nadm` — the permutations ENUMERATED and the ones that survived the
// constraint set. They are on the decision because the count is part of the
// justification: `nadm > JC_MAX_CAND()` is the one case where the plan refuses an
// order it proved legal, and a reader has to be able to see that it did.
//
// `cslot` / `conw` / `crole` / `cstrat` / `ckey` / `csel` / `cwhy` — the candidate
// table, `JC_MAX_FL()`-strided. For candidate `c` at floatable position `j`:
// WHICH FLOATABLE SOURCE sits there — by its QUERY index among the floatable
// sources, which is also the index of its size fact on the plan, so a candidate is
// a permutation and a `JCTable` row at once (`fl` maps it back to a chain slot) —
// then WHICH PREDICATE lands on it, the cost role, and the
// strategy triple that position's step runs under. The strategies are decided HERE
// and stamped onto the reordered chain rather than re-derived by the emitter: a
// moved step binds a different row var, so its equi-key and therefore its strategy
// are a different question from the one the planner already answered, and both
// answers come out of the one copy of the rule (`join_sel`).
//
// `rjn` — THE REFUSAL CENSUS, one count per rejection code (`RJ_*`). It exists
// because the ground a one-candidate outcome records used to be `first_rej` — the
// code of the FIRST permutation that failed — and with three or more sources the
// alternatives fail on DIFFERENT constraints: measured on `wql_gpath_e2e::all_ports`,
// 5 refused permutations, 2 on C5 and 3 on C7, and the plan carried the C5 sentence
// alone. The conclusion "no other order is admissible" is entailed by the
// CONJUNCTION of the refusals, so the plan records all of them with their counts, in
// CODE order — a fixed table — and not in the order the enumeration happened to
// visit them. That last part is the same argument the artifact bound rests on:
// choosing by enumeration order is not a decision.
//
// `deferred` — the nests exist, but the DISCRIMINANT is not a field of the
// prepared plan: the numbers only become free inside `run` (ADR 0024 S4j), so the
// comparison is made there, once, above the nest. `dynamic` still means "more than
// one nest is emitted" either way; `deferred` says WHICH of the two decision points
// filled the discriminant, and the emitter reads exactly that.
pub struct JoinOrder {
    pub dynamic:  bool,
    pub deferred: bool,
    pub ncand:    i64,
    pub nfl:      i64,
    pub nperm:    i64,
    pub nadm:     i64,
    pub rjn:      [i64; 8],
    pub why:      str,
    pub fl:       [i64; 4],
    pub cslot:    [i64; 16],
    pub conw:     [i64; 16],
    pub crole:    [i64; 16],
    pub cstrat:   [i32; 16],
    pub ckey:     [i32; 16],
    pub csel:     [i64; 16],
    pub cwhy:     [str; 16],
}

fn jo_fixed(why: str) -> JoinOrder {
    return JoinOrder { dynamic: false, deferred: false, ncand: 1i64, nfl: 0i64,
                       nperm: 0i64, nadm: 0i64, rjn: [0i64; 8], why: why,
                       fl: [0i64; 4],
                       cslot: [0i64; 16], conw: [0i64; 16], crole: [0i64; 16],
                       cstrat: [0i32; 16], ckey: [0i32; 16], csel: [-1i64; 16],
                       cwhy: ["", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", ""] };
}

/// A source's SIZE, as an expression evaluable in the PREPARE fn — that is,
/// before the query's own prelude has run (ADR 0024 S4i).
///
/// Two spellings, and the order matters. A source that DECLARED a size operation
/// has already been asked (`AccessPlan.size_at`, S4g) — the number is one
/// reporter call on the source PARAMETER, which `prepare` can make for itself;
/// `at` receives the rel index so the caller can emit that one binding and
/// nothing else. Anything else is asked the way the nest's own loop condition
/// asks: `.len()` on an indexable source.
///
/// ⚠ AND THAT SECOND SPELLING IS ONLY AVAILABLE ON A PARAMETER. A rel's slice
/// (`__rel_x_sl`) is a local of the generated fn, produced by materializing the
/// relation — so its length is not a fact about the input data but a result of
/// the query's own work, and a plan prepared before that work has no number to
/// compare. Such a side reports no size here, exactly like a STREAMED source
/// (whose length costs draining the iterator, the one thing streaming exists to
/// avoid).
fn size_expr_of(prm: &MacroParams, src: str, out: &mut String, at: &mut i64) -> bool {
    *at = -1i64;
    if prep_size_reason(prm, src) != SZ_OK() { return false; }
    let ri: i64 = prm.rel_find_src(src);
    if ri >= 0i64 {
        *at = ri;
        out.push_str("(");
        out.push_str(prm.rel_size_at[ri]);
        out.push_str(" as i64)");
        return true;
    }
    out.push_str("(");
    out.push_str(src);
    out.push_str(").len()");
    return true;
}

// ── WHY POINT (1) COULD OR COULD NOT MEASURE A SIDE (ADR 0024 S4m) ───────────
// The deferral is a decision about ONE SIDE AT A TIME that then binds all of them,
// and the ground it records has to say which side lost the number and which side
// merely came along. It did not: `admitted_why` asserted that BOTH sizes were
// "lengths of relations this query materializes anyway", which is false the moment
// one side is a PARAMETER — measured on `wql_deferred_plan_e2e::via_rel(ls, rs)`,
// whose `run` reads `(rs).len()` on a slice parameter `size_expr_of` measures
// happily. 2 of the corpus's 4 deferred queries carried that false ground.
//
// So the answer to "could `prepare` have had this one, and if not why not" is a CODE
// per side, produced by the one function `size_expr_of` itself consults, and both
// the plan's `why` and the trace compose their sentences from it. A hand-written
// sentence that happens to be true of the fixture in front of you is exactly what
// produced the defect.
fn SZ_OK() -> i32      { return 0i32; }
fn SZ_STREAMS() -> i32 { return 1i32; }
fn SZ_DERIVED() -> i32 { return 2i32; }

/// THE ONE RULE. `size_expr_of` returns true exactly when this returns `SZ_OK()`,
/// because it asks this and nothing else; `jo_report` asks it again to annotate the
/// trace, so the account and the code cannot name different reasons.
fn prep_size_reason(prm: &MacroParams, src: str) -> i32 {
    if prm.rel_src_streams(src) { return SZ_STREAMS(); }
    let ri: i64 = prm.rel_find_src(src);
    if ri >= 0i64 && prm.rel_size_at[ri].len() == 0i64 { return SZ_DERIVED(); }
    return SZ_OK();
}

/// Why point (1) has no number for this side — the failed antecedent, in the
/// vocabulary of the thing that failed.
fn sz_no_prepare_words(r: i32) -> str {
    if r == SZ_DERIVED() {
        return "is a relation THIS QUERY MATERIALIZES: the binding is a local of the generated fn, so before the query's own prelude has run there is no number to read and obtaining one would mean running the query a plan may not run";
    }
    if r == SZ_STREAMS() {
        return "STREAMS: an iterator has no length, and counting means draining it — the query's own work traded for a plan fact, which this axis refuses at both points";
    }
    // Default-DENY, stated: a code nobody has named yet does not get to borrow one
    // of the grounds above.
    return "has no size at point (1) for a reason this module has not named — the classification reached a code with no ground, which is a defect in `prep_size_reason` and not a property of the query";
}

/// What the side IS, for the account of the read that `run` actually makes. Both
/// answers are O(1) field reads (`run_size_expr_of` states the bound); they differ
/// in WHERE the field came from, which is the whole of what point (1) could not have.
fn sz_run_words(r: i32) -> str {
    if r == SZ_DERIVED() {
        return "a relation the prelude materialized for the query's own reasons";
    }
    if r == SZ_STREAMS() {
        return "a streamed source, which has no length here either — this side cannot be reached";
    }
    return "an input the caller passed in, which `prepare` COULD have measured";
}

/// A source's SIZE as an expression evaluable INSIDE `run` — in the region between
/// the prelude's materializations and the join's index build (ADR 0024 S4j, the
/// SECOND decision point).
///
/// ⚠ ONE SPELLING, and that is the point: `(<src>).len()`, which is the SAME
/// expression the nest's own base loop condition reads (`chain_nest_frag`). So the
/// number is not obtained, it is already there — a length field of a binding the
/// prelude made because the query needed it. That is what "free" means here, and
/// it is the only ground on which deferring is allowed to buy anything: nothing
/// about the query's cost moves.
///
/// ⚠ A STREAMED SIDE STILL HAS NO NUMBER. An iterator has no length, and draining
/// it to count is the query's own work — exactly what streaming exists to avoid,
/// and exactly the trade this axis forbids. Such a side reports none at BOTH
/// points, so its join keeps one nest and the refusal keeps its ground.
///
/// ⚠ AND THAT GUARD CANNOT FIRE TODAY, which is a fact worth writing down rather
/// than a reason to drop it. Streaming requires the ABSENCE of `order by`
/// (`plan_walker::plan_mark_single_pass`: a sort re-binds joined rows by position,
/// so every side must be indexable) and a transposition requires its PRESENCE — the
/// two conditions exclude each other, so every side reaching this function is
/// materialized. The check stays because the coupling is another module's decision
/// and a streaming order-preserving sort would end it silently; a function that is
/// right only because of a distant invariant it does not name is the phase-proxy
/// anti-pattern. `wql_deferred_plan_e2e` measures the consequence instead: an
/// ITERATOR producer whose length is free because the sort drained it anyway, with
/// the pull count pinning that the deferral added no second drain.
///
/// ⚠ A DECLARED SIZE REPORTER IS NOT CONSULTED HERE. At this point the rel is
/// materialized, so its length is the exact count rather than a reported estimate,
/// and it costs less than the call would. `size_expr_of` is the one that asks the
/// declaration, because before the query runs the declaration is all there is.
///
/// ⚠ THE BOUND THIS RESTS ON, STATED: `len()` MUST BE O(1). "The number is already
/// there" is not a figure of speech — it is the entire licence for deferring, and it
/// is a claim about the receiver, not about the spelling. Every source that reaches
/// this function today satisfies it structurally: a floatable side is either a
/// `&[T]` parameter or a rel binding the prelude materialized into a `Vec`, and both
/// answer `len()` from a field. A source whose `len()` walked its contents would make
/// the deferred decision cost a traversal per call — the query's own work traded for
/// a plan fact, which is the exact error this axis exists to refuse.
///
/// ⚠ AND THIS COMPILER CANNOT ASK. There is no capability that reports what a
/// receiver's `len()` costs — `declares_size` says a source HAS a reported size, not
/// what reading it costs, and `rel_src_streams` says it has none at all. So the
/// guarantee is carried by the two shapes above and by the fact that nothing else can
/// reach here, NOT by a check. The remedy the day a source arrives whose length is
/// computed rather than stored is a DECLARATION on the same channel as `size` — the
/// cost class of the length, asked here — and not a special case in this function.
/// Until then this is a declared limit with its ground, deliberately not silent.
///
/// ⚠ REPEATS ARE ELIMINATED SEPARATELY, and that is a different property from this
/// one: `rexpr_walk::push_size_bindings_4` binds one fact per distinct expression
/// TEXT, at both decision points, so a self-join asks its source's length once
/// rather than once per position. That removes the multiplier; it does not weaken the
/// O(1) requirement on the single read.
fn run_size_expr_of(prm: &MacroParams, src: str, out: &mut String) -> bool {
    if prm.rel_src_streams(src) { return false; }
    out.push_str("(");
    out.push_str(src);
    out.push_str(").len()");
    return true;
}

// ── Does a predicate / a source expression read a row var? ───────────────────
// `refs_mask` answers about ONE name at a time and bit 0 is "references it", so a
// dependency SET is that question asked per chain var. Written here rather than
// inlined because C4 and C5 both ask it and they must ask it the same way.
fn refs_var(w: i64, v: str) -> bool {
    if w == 0i64 { return false; }
    if v.len() == 0i64 { return false; }
    let e: SExpr = WAny::ref_to(w as *const u8).view::<SExpr>();
    return (refs_mask(e, v) & 1i32) != 0i32;
}

/// Does the SOURCE TEXT `s` name the row var `v` as its path root? A traversal
/// step's source is `d.emps` — a field path of an outer row — so the dependency is
/// in the text, not in a predicate.
///
/// ⚠ A PREFIX MATCH IS NOT ENOUGH: `d` must not match `depts`. The character after
/// the prefix has to be a non-identifier one, which is what makes `d.emps` a
/// reference to `d` and `depts` not.
fn src_refs_var(s: str, v: str) -> bool {
    if v.len() == 0i64 { return false; }
    if s.len() < v.len() { return false; }
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < v.len() {
        if str_byte_at(s, i) != str_byte_at(v, i) { return false; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    if s.len() == v.len() { return true; }
    let c: u8 = str_byte_at(s, v.len());
    if c >= 48u8 && c <= 57u8 { return false; }
    if c >= 65u8 && c <= 90u8 { return false; }
    if c >= 97u8 && c <= 122u8 { return false; }
    if c == 95u8 { return false; }
    return true;
}

/// The next permutation of `p[0..n)` in lexicographic order; false when `p` is the
/// last one. Lexicographic and not Heap's, because the enumeration must START at
/// the identity — candidate 0 has to be the query's own order, both so that a tie
/// keeps the rows where the query put them and so that the recorded strategies of
/// candidate 0 are the ones the planner already decided.
fn next_perm(p: &mut [i64; 4], n: i64) -> bool {
    if n < 2i64 { return false; }
    let mut i: i64 = n - 2i64;
    loop {
        if i < 0i64 { return false; }
        if p[i] < p[i + 1i64] { break; }
        i = i - 1i64;
    }
    let mut j: i64 = n - 1i64;
    while p[j] <= p[i] { j = j - 1i64; }
    let t: i64 = p[i];
    p[i] = p[j];
    p[j] = t;
    let mut a: i64 = i + 1i64;
    let mut b: i64 = n - 1i64;
    while a < b {
        let u: i64 = p[a];
        p[a] = p[b];
        p[b] = u;
        a = a + 1i64;
        b = b - 1i64;
    }
    return true;
}

// ── Rejection grounds ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A code per constraint, so the census the trace prints and the ground the plan
// records come from one table. `RJ_NONE` is admitted.
fn RJ_NONE() -> i32     { return 0i32; }
fn RJ_PINDEP() -> i32   { return 1i32; }
fn RJ_PREDBASE() -> i32 { return 2i32; }
fn RJ_PREDDUP() -> i32  { return 3i32; }
fn RJ_RESCAN() -> i32   { return 4i32; }
fn RJ_DEDUP() -> i32    { return 5i32; }
fn RJ_SHAPE() -> i32    { return 6i32; }
fn RJ_PREDPIN() -> i32  { return 7i32; }

fn rj_words(r: i32, nfl: i64) -> str {
    if r == RJ_PINDEP() {
        return "C4: a pinned step (an anti join or a traversal) would stand before a row var its `on` or its field-path source reads — the dependency is not bound yet at that depth";
    }
    if r == RJ_PREDBASE() {
        return "C5: a join predicate would land on the OUTERMOST loop, which carries none — nothing has bound its other side at that position";
    }
    if r == RJ_PREDDUP() {
        return "C5: two join predicates land on the same position and a step carries one `on` — this order would need a conjunction, which nothing here builds";
    }
    // ⚠ A SEPARATE GROUND, not a variant of the one above, because the antecedent
    // that failed is a different one: nothing collided here — the predicate's
    // last-bound var simply belongs to a step whose `on` is not a floating
    // predicate's to take. Naming the collision instead would send a reader looking
    // for a second predicate that does not exist.
    if r == RJ_PREDPIN() {
        return "C5: a join predicate's last-bound var belongs to a PINNED step (an anti join or a traversal), whose `on` is its own — nothing here re-attaches a floating predicate to one, so the predicate has no evaluable position in this order";
    }
    if r == RJ_RESCAN() {
        if nfl == 2i64 {
            return "the reverse direction has no equi-key that can be indexed, so driving the other side would rescan a source the query order reads once";
        }
        return "C6: this order has no indexable equi-key for a source the query's order reads exactly once, so it would rescan what the plan may already have left as a consumed iterator";
    }
    if r == RJ_DEDUP() {
        if nfl == 2i64 {
            return "both sides are the same source, so the two sizes are equal by construction — no measurement at either decision point can prefer one order, and the transposed nest could never be the one chosen";
        }
        return "C7: this order's (size, role) sequence is the one an earlier candidate already has, so it costs the same for every possible data and no measurement could ever select it";
    }
    if r == RJ_SHAPE() {
        return "a floatable step carries no `on` (a cross product), so the predicates and the positions are not in bijection and C5 has nothing to place";
    }
    return "admitted";
}

/// Render a candidate's row-var sequence, for the trace's census. The subject of a
/// per-candidate line has to name the ORDER, and an order is its sources.
fn push_seq(out: &mut String, c: &JCh, seq: &[i64; 9], np: i64) {
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < np {
        if i > 0i64 { out.push_str(">"); }
        out.push_str(c.var[seq[i]]);
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
}

// ── The derivation ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/// Enumerate the permutations of the floatable sources, apply C3–C7, and fill the
/// candidate table with the ones that survive (up to `JC_MAX_CAND()`).
///
/// Returns the rejection code of the FIRST challenger that failed — which is the
/// ground a one-candidate outcome records. The full census goes to the trace,
/// because "which orders were considered and why each loser lost" is the answer,
/// not a footnote to it: a single `why` naming the winner is the shape this slice
/// outgrew.
fn derive_orders(c: &JCh, t: &ElTypes, h: &Writ, szs: &mut [String; 4],
                 jo: &mut JoinOrder) -> i32 {
    let m: i64 = c.n + 1i64;
    // Floatable slots (base + classic steps) in QUERY order, and the pinned ones
    // with the number of floatable slots that preceded them (C3).
    let mut nfl: i64 = 0i64;
    let mut pin: [i64; 9] = [0i64; 9];
    let mut pbc: [i64; 9] = [0i64; 9];
    let mut npin: i64 = 0i64;
    let mut s: i64 = 0i64;
    while s < m {
        if s > 0i64 && (c.anti[s] || c.edge[s]) {
            pin[npin] = s;
            pbc[npin] = nfl;
            npin = npin + 1i64;
        } else {
            jo.fl[nfl] = s;
            nfl = nfl + 1i64;
        }
        s = s + 1i64;
    }
    jo.nfl = nfl;

    // The floating predicates: one per floatable STEP. The query's base carries
    // none, so there are nfl-1 of them and nfl-1 non-base floatable positions —
    // C5 is a bijection requirement, and a missing predicate breaks it before any
    // permutation is looked at.
    let mut fp: [i64; 4] = [0i64; 4];
    let mut nfp: i64 = 0i64;
    let mut k: i64 = 1i64;
    while k < nfl {
        let sl: i64 = jo.fl[k];
        if c.onw[sl] == 0i64 { return RJ_SHAPE(); }
        fp[nfp] = c.onw[sl];
        nfp = nfp + 1i64;
        k = k + 1i64;
    }

    // Which floatable sources the query itself reads exactly once (C6): the base
    // is read once by construction, a step only under a build-once strategy.
    let mut once0: [bool; 4] = [false; 4];
    let mut q: i64 = 0i64;
    while q < nfl {
        let sl: i64 = jo.fl[q];
        if sl == 0i64 { once0[q] = true; } else { once0[q] = js_reads_once(c.strat[sl]); }
        q = q + 1i64;
    }

    let mut perm: [i64; 4] = [0i64, 1i64, 2i64, 3i64];
    let mut first_rej: i32 = RJ_NONE();
    jo.ncand = 0i64;
    jo.nadm = 0i64;
    jo.nperm = 0i64;
    loop {
        jo.nperm = jo.nperm + 1i64;
        let ident: bool = jo.nperm == 1i64;

        // 1. The candidate's full slot sequence: floatable in permuted order, each
        //    pinned step re-inserted after exactly as many floatable slots as
        //    preceded it in the query (C3).
        let mut seq: [i64; 9] = [0i64; 9];
        let mut pos: [i64; 9] = [0i64; 9];
        let mut np: i64 = 0i64;
        let mut j: i64 = 0i64;
        while j < nfl {
            seq[np] = jo.fl[perm[j]];
            np = np + 1i64;
            let mut pi: i64 = 0i64;
            while pi < npin {
                if pbc[pi] == j + 1i64 { seq[np] = pin[pi]; np = np + 1i64; }
                pi = pi + 1i64;
            }
            j = j + 1i64;
        }
        let mut pp: i64 = 0i64;
        while pp < np { pos[seq[pp]] = pp; pp = pp + 1i64; }
        // The floatable position of each floatable slot in THIS candidate.
        let mut flpos: [i64; 9] = [0i64; 9];
        let mut fj: i64 = 0i64;
        while fj < nfl { flpos[jo.fl[perm[fj]]] = fj; fj = fj + 1i64; }

        let mut rej: i32 = RJ_NONE();

        // 2. C4 — every pinned step's dependencies precede it.
        let mut pk: i64 = 0i64;
        while pk < npin && rej == RJ_NONE() {
            let ps: i64 = pin[pk];
            let mut dv: i64 = 0i64;
            while dv < np {
                let os: i64 = seq[dv];
                if os != ps {
                    let mut dep: bool = refs_var(c.onw[ps], c.var[os]);
                    if !dep && c.edge[ps] { dep = src_refs_var(c.src[ps], c.var[os]); }
                    if dep && pos[os] > pos[ps] { rej = RJ_PINDEP(); }
                }
                dv = dv + 1i64;
            }
            pk = pk + 1i64;
        }

        // 3. C5 — place each floating predicate at the LAST of the vars it reads.
        let mut land: [i64; 4] = [-1i64, -1i64, -1i64, -1i64];
        let mut slotpred: [i64; 4] = [0i64; 4];
        if rej == RJ_NONE() {
            let mut fi: i64 = 0i64;
            while fi < nfp {
                let mut best: i64 = -1i64;
                let mut dv2: i64 = 0i64;
                while dv2 < np {
                    let os2: i64 = seq[dv2];
                    if refs_var(fp[fi], c.var[os2]) {
                        if pos[os2] > best { best = pos[os2]; }
                    }
                    dv2 = dv2 + 1i64;
                }
                land[fi] = best;
                fi = fi + 1i64;
            }
            let mut fi2: i64 = 0i64;
            while fi2 < nfp && rej == RJ_NONE() {
                let lp: i64 = land[fi2];
                if lp <= 0i64 { rej = RJ_PREDBASE(); }
                else {
                    let ls: i64 = seq[lp];
                    if c.anti[ls] || c.edge[ls] { rej = RJ_PREDPIN(); }
                    else {
                        let fjx: i64 = flpos[ls];
                        if fjx == 0i64 { rej = RJ_PREDBASE(); }
                        else if slotpred[fjx] != 0i64 { rej = RJ_PREDDUP(); }
                        else { slotpred[fjx] = fp[fi2]; }
                    }
                }
                fi2 = fi2 + 1i64;
            }
        }

        // 4. Strategies + roles. Candidate 0 READS the plan's own decisions; a
        //    challenger asks the one copy of the rule (`join_sel`) about the pair
        //    it actually forms, because the moved step's equi-key is selected
        //    against a different new-side.
        let mut cst: [i32; 4] = [0i32; 4];
        let mut cky: [i32; 4] = [0i32; 4];
        let mut csl: [i64; 4] = [-1i64; 4];
        let mut cwy: [str; 4] = ["", "", "", ""];
        let mut crl: [i64; 4] = [0i64; 4];
        if rej == RJ_NONE() {
            crl[0] = JC_SCAN();
            let mut sj: i64 = 1i64;
            while sj < nfl && rej == RJ_NONE() {
                let sl2: i64 = jo.fl[perm[sj]];
                if ident {
                    cst[sj] = c.strat[sl2];
                    cky[sj] = c.key[sl2];
                    csl[sj] = c.sel[sl2];
                    cwy[sj] = c.swhy[sl2];
                    // ⚠ The derivation must REPRODUCE the query for the identity
                    // permutation, and the one place that could silently differ is
                    // C5's placement. If it does, the chain has a shape this
                    // module does not model and no order is offered.
                    if slotpred[sj] != c.onw[sl2] { rej = RJ_SHAPE(); }
                } else {
                    let pw: WRef<SExpr> = WRef::<SExpr>::from_any(WAny::ref_to(slotpred[sj] as *const u8));
                    let ss: StepSel = decide_join_step(pw, c.var[sl2], t, h);
                    cst[sj] = ss.strat;
                    cky[sj] = ss.key_ty;
                    csl[sj] = ss.sel;
                    cwy[sj] = ss.why;
                }
                if rej == RJ_NONE() {
                    if js_reads_once(cst[sj]) { crl[sj] = JC_INDEX(); } else { crl[sj] = JC_RESCAN(); }
                }
                sj = sj + 1i64;
            }
        }

        // 5. C6 — no source the query read once may become rescanned.
        if rej == RJ_NONE() && !ident {
            let mut ck: i64 = 1i64;
            while ck < nfl {
                if once0[perm[ck]] && !js_reads_once(cst[ck]) { rej = RJ_RESCAN(); }
                ck = ck + 1i64;
            }
        }

        // 6. C7 — a candidate whose (size, role) sequence an earlier one already
        //    has cannot be cheaper for any data.
        if rej == RJ_NONE() && !ident {
            let mut cc: i64 = 0i64;
            while cc < jo.ncand && rej == RJ_NONE() {
                let bb: i64 = cc * JC_MAX_FL();
                let mut same: bool = true;
                let mut dj: i64 = 0i64;
                while dj < nfl {
                    let a_ix: i64 = perm[dj];
                    let b_ix: i64 = jo.cslot[bb + dj];
                    if crl[dj] != jo.crole[bb + dj] { same = false; }
                    else if !str_eq(string_as_str(&szs[a_ix]), string_as_str(&szs[b_ix])) { same = false; }
                    dj = dj + 1i64;
                }
                if same { rej = RJ_DEDUP(); }
                cc = cc + 1i64;
            }
        }

        // 7. Record. An admissible order is COUNTED even when the artifact bound
        //    stops it being carried — that count is what the fallback reports.
        let mut sb: String = String::new();
        push_seq(&mut sb, c, &seq, np);
        if rej == RJ_NONE() {
            jo.nadm = jo.nadm + 1i64;
            if jo.ncand < JC_MAX_CAND() {
                let base: i64 = jo.ncand * JC_MAX_FL();
                let mut wj: i64 = 0i64;
                while wj < nfl {
                    jo.cslot[base + wj]  = perm[wj];
                    jo.conw[base + wj]   = slotpred[wj];
                    jo.crole[base + wj]  = crl[wj];
                    jo.cstrat[base + wj] = cst[wj];
                    jo.ckey[base + wj]   = cky[wj];
                    jo.csel[base + wj]   = csl[wj];
                    jo.cwhy[base + wj]   = cwy[wj];
                    wj = wj + 1i64;
                }
                jo.ncand = jo.ncand + 1i64;
                plan_trace(string_as_str(&sb), "order admitted", c.var[0],
                           "every constraint holds and the (size, role) sequence is new — this nest is emitted and priced at run time");
            } else {
                plan_trace(string_as_str(&sb), "order admitted but not carried", c.var[0],
                           "legal, and beyond the artifact's nest bound — see the summary");
            }
        } else {
            if first_rej == RJ_NONE() { first_rej = rej; }
            // ⚠ COUNTED, not just remembered. `first_rej` alone made the plan's own
            // ground a function of the enumeration order; the census is what lets the
            // verdict be the conjunction the conclusion actually rests on.
            let rc: i64 = rej as i64;
            jo.rjn[rc] = jo.rjn[rc] + 1i64;
            plan_trace(string_as_str(&sb), "order refused", c.var[0], rj_words(rej, nfl));
        }
        if !next_perm(&mut perm, nfl) { break; }
    }
    return first_rej;
}

// ── The entry ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/// Decide the set of join orders a query's body carries.
///
/// `has_sort` + `key_tn` are C1's antecedent, and BOTH halves of it: the `order by`
/// key's LOGOS TYPE NAME is passed because a sort licenses a second nest only if
/// the emitted comparison realizes the order the query named, and the caller
/// already computes that name 100 lines before it asks (`infer_ty_name(key, ty)`).
///
/// ⚠ THE NAME AND NOT THE CLASS, and that is a fix rather than a convenience: the
/// four-value EL class collapses every integer width onto INT, so `u64` and `i64`
/// arrived here indistinguishable and a u64 key kept a licence over a comparison
/// that sorts by its signed reinterpretation. `key_tn` is unread when `has_sort`
/// is false.
///
/// `whyb` is a CALLER-OWNED buffer for the one verdict whose ground is composed
/// rather than a literal (C1's key-order refusal quotes `el`'s answer). ⚠ It must
/// outlive this call the same way `szs` does: `jo.why` is not only traced, it
/// becomes a STRING LITERAL IN THE GENERATED PLAN (`prep.why` → `push_str_lit`),
/// so a buffer local to this fn would be freed before the artifact is written. Do
/// not touch it between this call and emission.
///
/// `szs`/`ats` receive one entry per FLOATABLE source in QUERY order: the size as
/// an expression, and the rel whose declared reporter it calls (-1 for a
/// parameter's own `.len()`), so the PREPARE fn can bind exactly the reporters its
/// comparison reads. The comparison itself is NOT built here and not built per
/// query: it is `logos.std.wql.join_cost::jc_order_pick` over the candidate table,
/// called from the emitted `prepare` for a prepared decision (which is also where
/// its result is recorded on the plan, so the branch reads the plan's field and
/// cannot disagree with the number the plan reports, ADR 0024 S4i) or from the one
/// deferred binding above the nest for a deferred one (S4j). When `deferred` comes
/// back true, every `ats` entry is -1 and the expressions in `szs` are to be
/// evaluated in `run`, not in `prepare`.
pub fn decide_join_orders(c: &JCh, has_sort: bool, key_tn: str, t: &ElTypes,
                          prm: &MacroParams, h: &Writ,
                          szs: &mut [String; 4], ats: &mut [i64; 4],
                          whyb: &mut String) -> JoinOrder {
    let mut jo: JoinOrder = jo_fixed("");
    let base_var: str = c.var[0];
    let s0_var: str = c.var[1];
    if c.n < 1i64 {
        return jo_fixed("no join step — there is no second side to drive from");
    }
    // ⚠ THE FIRST STEP'S PIN, ahead of the derivation and on purpose — see the
    // header. The general constraint C4 reaches the same conclusion on every shape
    // in the corpus; keeping the specific antecedent is what tells a reader WHICH
    // property of their query stopped the reorder.
    if c.edge[1] {
        jo = jo_fixed("a traversal step's source is a field path of an outer row, so it cannot be an outermost loop — there is nothing to bind its base to");
    } else if c.anti[1] {
        jo = jo_fixed("an anti join is not symmetric: the rows of the base with no match are a different question from the rows of the step with no match");
    } else if !has_sort {
        jo = jo_fixed("the row order of a join IS the nest's, and this query names no other one — without `order by` there is no sequence to restore, so driving the other side would return the same rows in a different order");
    } else if !el_order_faithful(key_tn) {
        // C1's SECOND antecedent. `order by` is present, so a sequence is named —
        // but the sort restores THAT sequence only if the comparison the backend
        // emits for this key realizes it, and this key's does not. Refused with its
        // ground rather than redefining what the sort means for this key: that
        // would move the answer of single-nest queries, which no reorder decision
        // is entitled to do.
        whyb.clear();
        whyb.push_str("`order by` names a sequence, but C1's licence is that the EMITTED comparison REALIZES that sequence, not that a key exists — and being total is one of that licence's conditions rather than the whole of it. This key (`");
        whyb.push_str(key_tn);
        whyb.push_str("`) fails it: ");
        whyb.push_str(el_order_why(key_tn));
        whyb.push_str(". The row-index tiebreak that makes every nest yield the same sequence rests on that comparison, so with this key the answer would stay a fact about WHICH NEST collected the tuples — this chain keeps the query's own order");
        jo = jo_fixed(string_as_str(whyb));
    } else {
        // C2 — for EVERY step, not only the first: a chain with one undecided step
        // has no recorded strategy to reason from at that position, and C6 is a
        // claim about the strategies the planner chose.
        let mut und: bool = false;
        let mut si: i64 = 1i64;
        while si <= c.n {
            if !c.edge[si] && c.strat[si] == JS_NONE() { und = true; }
            si = si + 1i64;
        }
        if und {
            jo = jo_fixed("no plan decided this step's strategy (a rel body's own chain is emitted per fixpoint variant), so there is no decision to offer a second order for");
        } else {
            jo = decide_over_set(c, t, prm, h, szs, ats, whyb);
        }
    }
    jo_report(&jo, c, base_var, s0_var, prm);
    return jo;
}

/// The size facts, then the derivation, then the verdict — in that order, because
/// the derivation's C7 compares SIZE EXPRESSIONS and cannot run before they exist.
///
/// ⚠ `whyb` IS THE CALLER'S BUFFER AND BOTH COMPOSED VERDICTS LIVE IN IT. Two of the
/// three grounds this fn can record are now DERIVED rather than written down — the
/// deferred admission names which side lost its number at point (1), and a refusal
/// names every constraint that refused rather than the first — and both are built
/// text. `jo.why` becomes a string LITERAL in the generated plan, so the buffer must
/// outlive this call; the two paths are mutually exclusive, so one buffer suffices.
fn decide_over_set(c: &JCh, t: &ElTypes, prm: &MacroParams, h: &Writ,
                   szs: &mut [String; 4], ats: &mut [i64; 4],
                   whyb: &mut String) -> JoinOrder {
    // How many floatable sources, and is that within the plan's fact table?
    let m: i64 = c.n + 1i64;
    let mut nfl: i64 = 0i64;
    let mut fl: [i64; 9] = [0i64; 9];
    let mut s: i64 = 0i64;
    while s < m {
        if !(s > 0i64 && (c.anti[s] || c.edge[s])) {
            if nfl < 9i64 { fl[nfl] = s; }
            nfl = nfl + 1i64;
        }
        s = s + 1i64;
    }
    if nfl < 2i64 {
        return jo_fixed("only one source is free to move — every other step is pinned (an anti join or a traversal), so there is no second order to compare");
    }
    if nfl > JC_MAX_FL() {
        return jo_fixed("more floatable sources than a plan holds facts for (four): the permutation search and the plan's size table are both bounded there, so this chain keeps the query's own order rather than searching a truncated space");
    }

    // ── The size facts, one per floatable source, at ONE decision point ──────
    // ⚠ ALL OF THEM AT THE SAME POINT. A cost that mixed a fact `prepare` can hold
    // with one only `run` can have would be a plan that cannot be prepared, so the
    // reusable point is tried for every source first and the deferral is the
    // fallback — never the preference.
    //
    // ⚠ AND THE PER-SIDE ANSWER IS KEPT, not collapsed into one boolean. `okp` says
    // the deferral is forced; `rsn` says BY WHICH SIDE, which is the difference
    // between the ground this plan records and the ground it used to record.
    let mut okp: bool = true;
    let mut rsn: [i32; 4] = [0i32; 4];
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < nfl {
        szs[i].clear();
        rsn[i] = prep_size_reason(prm, c.src[fl[i]]);
        if !size_expr_of(prm, c.src[fl[i]], &mut szs[i], &mut ats[i]) { okp = false; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    let mut deferred: bool = false;
    if !okp {
        let mut okr: bool = true;
        let mut i2: i64 = 0i64;
        while i2 < nfl {
            szs[i2].clear();
            ats[i2] = -1i64;
            if !run_size_expr_of(prm, c.src[fl[i2]], &mut szs[i2]) { okr = false; }
            i2 = i2 + 1i64;
        }
        if !okr {
            let mut i3: i64 = 0i64;
            while i3 < nfl { szs[i3].clear(); ats[i3] = -1i64; i3 = i3 + 1i64; }
            return jo_fixed("a side's size is free at NEITHER decision point: it streams, so it has no length before the query runs and none inside it either — counting would mean draining the iterator, which is the query's own work, and trading that for a plan fact is what this axis refuses");
        }
        deferred = true;
    }

    let mut jo: JoinOrder = jo_fixed("");
    let rej: i32 = derive_orders(c, t, h, szs, &mut jo);
    if jo.nadm > JC_MAX_CAND() {
        // ⚠ THE STATED FALLBACK. Carrying "the first four" would mean choosing
        // three challengers by enumeration order, which is not a decision, and
        // dropping the rest without saying so is the failure the bound exists to
        // make visible. So the plan declines the whole reorder and reports the
        // count — the remedy is a smaller chain or an artifact that can hold one
        // order per object file.
        //
        // ⚠ THE COUNT IS ON THE TRACE, NOT IN THIS STRING. `why` travels onto the
        // plan as a string LITERAL in generated source and outlives the compile; a
        // `str` built from a local `String` here would be a view into a buffer this
        // function is about to drop. The `order search` line carries the numbers.
        let mut r: JoinOrder = jo_fixed("more admissible orders than an artifact carries nests (four): the reorder is declined whole rather than searched over a set chosen by enumeration order, so the query's own order is emitted — the `order search` trace line reports how many were proved legal");
        r.nfl = jo.nfl;
        r.nperm = jo.nperm;
        r.nadm = jo.nadm;
        let mut cy: i64 = 0i64;
        while cy < 8i64 { r.rjn[cy] = jo.rjn[cy]; cy = cy + 1i64; }
        let mut z: i64 = 0i64;
        while z < 4i64 { szs[z].clear(); ats[z] = -1i64; z = z + 1i64; }
        return r;
    }
    if jo.ncand < 2i64 {
        // ⚠ THE GROUND IS THE WHOLE CENSUS WHERE THE CENSUS HAS MORE THAN ONE ENTRY.
        // One distinct ground records that ground verbatim — there is nothing to
        // choose between, and the pair case's own sentences are reached exactly as
        // before. Two or more and the plain `rj_words(first_rej)` would be a verdict
        // selected by enumeration order, which is what the artifact bound already
        // refuses to do with candidates.
        let mut r2: JoinOrder = jo_fixed(rj_words(rej, jo.nfl));
        if rj_distinct(&jo) > 1i64 {
            whyb.clear();
            push_census_why(whyb, &jo);
            r2 = jo_fixed(string_as_str(whyb));
        }
        r2.nfl = jo.nfl;
        r2.nperm = jo.nperm;
        r2.nadm = jo.nadm;
        let mut cz: i64 = 0i64;
        while cz < 8i64 { r2.rjn[cz] = jo.rjn[cz]; cz = cz + 1i64; }
        let mut z2: i64 = 0i64;
        while z2 < 4i64 { szs[z2].clear(); ats[z2] = -1i64; z2 = z2 + 1i64; }
        return r2;
    }
    jo.dynamic = true;
    jo.deferred = deferred;
    if deferred {
        whyb.clear();
        push_defer_why(whyb, c, &fl, nfl, jo.ncand, &rsn);
        jo.why = string_as_str(whyb);
    } else {
        jo.why = admitted_why(jo.ncand);
    }
    return jo;
}

/// The ground an ADMITTED PREPARED set records. Two candidates over two sources is
/// the pair case and keeps the pair case's sentence, because it is still exactly what
/// happened: the model's answer for k = 2 is "index the smaller side".
///
/// ⚠ THE DEFERRED HALF IS NOT HERE ANY MORE, and that is the fix: its ground depends
/// on WHICH SIDE lost its number at point (1), so it cannot be a constant. See
/// `push_defer_why`.
fn admitted_why(ncand: i64) -> str {
    if ncand == 2i64 {
        return "either side may drive: the plan indexes the SMALLER side and walks the larger, decided once in `prepare` from the sizes the plan asked for — the sort's tuple tiebreak makes both nests yield the same sequence";
    }
    return "more than two orders are admissible: every one of them is emitted as its own nest and `join_cost` prices them all in `prepare` from the sizes the plan asked for — the sort's tuple tiebreak makes every nest yield the same sequence, and `cost_of(ix)` reports what each one would have cost";
}

/// The ground a DEFERRED admission records, COMPOSED FROM THE PER-SIDE ANSWERS the
/// deferral was decided on.
///
/// ⚠ WHAT THE OLD SENTENCE GOT WRONG, precisely: it said both sizes were lengths of
/// relations this query materializes anyway, and "not on any fact `prepare` can
/// hold". For `via_rel(ls, rs)` one of the two is `(rs).len()` on a slice PARAMETER —
/// a fact `prepare` holds happily, and `size_expr_of` measures it at point (1)
/// without complaint. A reader was told no number was obtainable when one of two was.
///
/// ⚠ SO THE SENTENCE NAMES BOTH GROUPS AND WHY THE SECOND ONE MOVED. The accurate
/// antecedent is the one already written above the size loop: ALL OF THEM AT THE SAME
/// POINT. A cost that mixed a fact from point (1) with one from point (2) would be a
/// plan that cannot be prepared, so one unmeasurable side sends every side to point
/// (2) — and a side that came along is a different fact about a query than a side
/// that forced the move. The remedy differs too: declaring a `size` on the source
/// that lost its number would move the whole decision back to point (1), which is
/// advice only a reader who was told which source that is can act on.
///
/// ⚠ ONE SENTENCE PER DISTINCT SOURCE, not per position. A self-join binds the same
/// source at two or three positions and repeating its ground once per position is
/// noise a reader has to diff — the same reason `push_size_bindings_4` binds one fact
/// per distinct expression TEXT rather than one per slot. The count is kept, because
/// "this source supplies three of the four numbers" is a fact about the query.
fn defer_first_at(c: &JCh, fl: &[i64; 9], k: i64) -> i64 {
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < k {
        if str_eq(c.src[fl[i]], c.src[fl[k]]) { return i; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return k;
}

fn defer_count_at(c: &JCh, fl: &[i64; 9], nfl: i64, k: i64) -> i64 {
    let mut n: i64 = 0i64;
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < nfl {
        if str_eq(c.src[fl[i]], c.src[fl[k]]) { n = n + 1i64; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return n;
}

fn push_defer_src(out: &mut String, c: &JCh, fl: &[i64; 9], nfl: i64, k: i64) {
    out.push_str("`");
    out.push_str(c.src[fl[k]]);
    out.push_str("`");
    let n: i64 = defer_count_at(c, fl, nfl, k);
    if n > 1i64 {
        out.push_str(" (");
        out.push_i64(n);
        out.push_str(" positions)");
    }
}

fn push_defer_why(out: &mut String, c: &JCh, fl: &[i64; 9], nfl: i64,
                  ncand: i64, rsn: &[i32; 4]) {
    if ncand == 2i64 {
        out.push_str("either side may drive");
    } else {
        out.push_str("one of ");
        out.push_i64(ncand);
        out.push_str(" orders drives");
    }
    out.push_str(", and not on a fact `prepare` can hold, because ");
    let mut nno: i64 = 0i64;
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < nfl {
        if rsn[i] != SZ_OK() && defer_first_at(c, fl, i) == i {
            if nno > 0i64 { out.push_str("; "); }
            push_defer_src(out, c, fl, nfl, i);
            out.push_str(" ");
            out.push_str(sz_no_prepare_words(rsn[i]));
            nno = nno + 1i64;
        }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    // ⚠ AND IF NO SIDE FAILED, SAY SO RATHER THAN EMITTING A SENTENCE WITH A HOLE.
    // `deferred` is set only where `size_expr_of` refused at least one side, so this
    // is unreachable — and an unreachable branch that would produce broken prose is
    // still a `why` a reader could be shown, so it names the contradiction instead.
    if nno == 0i64 {
        out.push_str("the deferral was taken with every side measurable at point (1), which contradicts the condition that sets it — this is a defect in `decide_over_set`, not a property of the query");
    }
    // The sides point (1) COULD have measured. Naming them is the whole correction:
    // their presence is why "no fact was obtainable" was false, and their movement to
    // point (2) has a ground of its own.
    let mut nok: i64 = 0i64;
    let mut j: i64 = 0i64;
    while j < nfl {
        if rsn[j] == SZ_OK() && defer_first_at(c, fl, j) == j {
            if nok > 0i64 { out.push_str(", "); }
            else { out.push_str(". `prepare` COULD have measured "); }
            push_defer_src(out, c, fl, nfl, j);
            nok = nok + 1i64;
        }
        j = j + 1i64;
    }
    if nok > 0i64 {
        if nok == 1i64 { out.push_str(" — that side is read in `run` all the same"); }
        else           { out.push_str(" — those sides are read in `run` all the same"); }
        out.push_str(", because a cost may not mix a fact from point (1) with one from point (2): every size in one comparison comes from the one point at which every one of them is free, and one unmeasurable side is enough to choose which point that is. Declaring a `size` operation on the source(s) above would move the whole decision back to `prepare`");
    }
    out.push_str(". So the order is decided inside `run` — once per call, above the nest, from lengths that are one field read where they stand. ");
    out.push_str(defer_not_held());
}

/// How many DISTINCT rejection grounds the census holds. `RJ_NONE` is admission and
/// is never counted.
fn rj_distinct(jo: &JoinOrder) -> i64 {
    let mut n: i64 = 0i64;
    let mut k: i64 = 1i64;
    while k < 8i64 {
        if jo.rjn[k] > 0i64 { n = n + 1i64; }
        k = k + 1i64;
    }
    return n;
}

/// The verdict a MULTI-GROUND refusal records: every ground, with its count, in CODE
/// order.
///
/// ⚠ CODE ORDER, NOT ENUMERATION ORDER, and not "the first". `all_ports` in
/// `wql_gpath_e2e` refuses 5 permutations — 2 by C5, 3 by C7 — and the plan used to
/// carry the C5 sentence alone because C5 happened to fire on the first challenger.
/// The conclusion "the query's own order is the only one left" is entailed by the
/// CONJUNCTION: remove either ground and an order survives. A justification that
/// names one antecedent of a conjunction is a justification a reader cannot check.
fn push_census_why(out: &mut String, jo: &JoinOrder) {
    out.push_str("no other order is admissible, and that conclusion rests on ALL of the refusals rather than on the first one: ");
    out.push_i64(jo.nperm - jo.nadm);
    out.push_str(" of ");
    out.push_i64(jo.nperm);
    out.push_str(" permutations were refused, on ");
    out.push_i64(rj_distinct(jo));
    out.push_str(" distinct grounds — ");
    let mut w: i64 = 0i64;
    let mut k: i64 = 1i64;
    while k < 8i64 {
        if jo.rjn[k] > 0i64 {
            if w > 0i64 { out.push_str("; "); }
            out.push_i64(jo.rjn[k]);
            out.push_str("x [");
            out.push_str(rj_words(k as i32, jo.nfl));
            out.push_str("]");
            w = w + 1i64;
        }
        k = k + 1i64;
    }
    out.push_str(". Every one of them had to hold for the query's own order to be the only one left, so the remedy is whichever of them your query can lose — not the first one listed");
}

// ── The one decision channel ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Recorded at the moment of deciding, like every other plan decision: a returned
// "fixed" has already lost the antecedent that fixed it.
//
// ⚠ THE VERDICT NAMES THE DECISION POINT, not just the outcome. "drive either
// side" and "drive either side in `run`" are two different claims about a plan —
// the first is reusable, the second is remade every call — and a reader must be
// able to tell them apart from the trace alone. Beyond two candidates the verdict
// names the COUNT, because "either" stops being true.
fn jo_report(jo: &JoinOrder, c: &JCh, base_var: str, s0_var: str, prm: &MacroParams) {
    let mut verdict: str = "drive fixed";
    let mut vb: String = String::new();
    if jo.dynamic {
        if jo.ncand == 2i64 {
            verdict = "drive either side";
            if jo.deferred { verdict = "drive either side in `run`"; }
        } else {
            vb.push_str("drive 1 of ");
            vb.push_i64(jo.ncand);
            if jo.deferred { vb.push_str(" in `run`"); }
            verdict = string_as_str(&vb);
        }
    }
    plan_trace(base_var, verdict, s0_var, jo.why);

    // The SEARCH's own account: how big the space was, how much of it is legal,
    // and how much the artifact carries. Printed for every chain that reached the
    // derivation, admitted or not, because "nothing was considered" and "four were
    // considered and three lost" are different answers.
    if jo.nperm > 0i64 {
        let mut sb: String = String::new();
        sb.push_i64(jo.nperm);
        sb.push_str(" permutations of ");
        sb.push_i64(jo.nfl);
        sb.push_str(" floatable sources enumerated, ");
        sb.push_i64(jo.nadm);
        sb.push_str(" admissible, ");
        sb.push_i64(jo.ncand);
        sb.push_str(" carried as nests; ");
        sb.push_str(jc_model_words());
        plan_trace(base_var, "order search", s0_var, string_as_str(&sb));
    }

    if jo.deferred {
        // WHICH RELATION SUPPLIES EACH NUMBER. The plan's own facts are -1 for a
        // deferred decision (nothing was measured when it was prepared), so the
        // expressions are the only record of what will be compared and where it
        // comes from. Rebuilt through the same rule that produced them, so the
        // trace cannot name one thing while the code reads another.
        //
        // ⚠ AND EACH IS ANNOTATED WITH WHAT IT IS. "each is the length of a binding
        // the prelude already made" was false for a side that is a PARAMETER — the
        // prelude made nothing of it, the caller passed it in, and `prepare` could
        // have measured it. The annotation comes from `prep_size_reason`, the same
        // function `size_expr_of` consulted to decide the deferral, so the trace
        // cannot describe a side differently from the way the decision classified it.
        let mut sb2: String = String::new();
        sb2.push_str("compared in `run` after the prelude, before the join's index: ");
        let m: i64 = c.n + 1i64;
        let mut nn: i64 = 0i64;
        let mut s: i64 = 0i64;
        while s < m {
            if !(s > 0i64 && (c.anti[s] || c.edge[s])) {
                if nn > 0i64 { sb2.push_str(" vs "); }
                let _t: bool = run_size_expr_of(prm, c.src[s], &mut sb2);
                sb2.push_str(" [");
                sb2.push_str(sz_run_words(prep_size_reason(prm, c.src[s])));
                sb2.push_str("]");
                nn = nn + 1i64;
            }
            s = s + 1i64;
        }
        sb2.push_str(" — every one of them is one field read where it stands, so the per-row cost does not move; they are read at the SAME point because a cost may not mix a fact from point (1) with one from point (2)");
        plan_trace(base_var, "sizes deferred to run", s0_var, string_as_str(&sb2));
    }

    // Every CARRIED challenger's own strategy decisions. The reverse direction is
    // a different question from the one the planner answered, and the answer is
    // recorded rather than left to the emitter to re-derive.
    if jo.dynamic {
        let mut cc: i64 = 1i64;
        while cc < jo.ncand {
            let b: i64 = cc * JC_MAX_FL();
            let nb_var: str = c.var[jo.fl[jo.cslot[b]]];
            let mut j: i64 = 1i64;
            while j < jo.nfl {
                let mut ab: String = String::new();
                ab.push_str(js_name(jo.cstrat[b + j]));
                ab.push_str(" when driven from ");
                ab.push_str(nb_var);
                plan_trace(c.var[jo.fl[jo.cslot[b + j]]], string_as_str(&ab), "",
                           jo.cwhy[b + j]);
                j = j + 1i64;
            }
            cc = cc + 1i64;
        }
    }
}
